window.PORTFOLIO_INDEX = [{"title":"About","category":"Pages","keywords":"About","text":"Sahana Sharma, Singapore, IB Diploma student, data science, economics, Algoverse and Solivia.","url":"index.html"},{"title":"About Sahana Sharma","category":"About","keywords":"IBDP data scientist economist founder Singapore","url":"index.html#about","text":"✦ hello, I’m Sahana Curious by nature. Quantitative by choice. An IB Diploma student , aspiring data scientist and economist, building where data, economics and technology meet. From machine-learning research to startup decision-making, I like asking better questions and making useful things. Explore my research ↗ My GitHub ↗ Singapore ✧ Mathematics AA · Economics · Computer Science a work in progress, always curious ✦ Sahana Sharma ♡ currently connecting models + real-world decisions ↗ ✧ ✳"},{"title":"My story: economics, data and building","category":"About","keywords":"Solivia co founder Algoverse internship","url":"index.html#my-story","text":"a little about me Numbers are the start. Better decisions are the point. I’m studying Mathematics AA HL, Economics HL and Computer Science HL at Westbourne College Singapore. I’m drawn to problems that need more than one way of thinking. As a Co-Founder of Solivia Pte. Ltd. , I built the company website, conduct market and commercial research, and support financial management. That work motivates my passion project: a transparent commercial decision-support system. With Algoverse , I work on machine-learning research involving sequence data, high-dimensional embeddings, model evaluation and attention-based approaches. My professional exposure also includes data operations and job shadowing across Pinterest, TikTok and Snapchat . More about my experience ↗"},{"title":"Research","category":"Pages","keywords":"Research","text":"The Solivia passion project, Algoverse machine learning and planned economics research.","url":"research.html"},{"title":"Solivia Commercial Decision Support System","category":"Research","keywords":"passion project decision intelligence suite pricing procurement forecasting risk statistics mathematics MR","url":"research.html#solivia-research","text":"Independent passion project In development Solivia Commercial Decision Support System Pricing, procurement, distributor-opportunity and supply-risk analytics for a healthcare distribution startup. Previously introduced in my portfolio as the Solivia Decision Intelligence Suite , this strand now has a defined three-phase plan. The goal is to move from scattered inputs to transparent, testable decisions . SQL & data design Cost & margin modelling Forecast evaluation Constrained optimisation Read the project write-up ↗ Try the scenario lab ↗ the research question How can a healthcare distribution startup make more defensible pricing and procurement decisions under uncertainty? 01 Working commercial system 02 Forecasting & optimisation 03 Written technical case study The linked write-up separates planned methods, synthetic demonstrations and outcomes still to be measured."},{"title":"Algoverse: Sequence-Modelling Benchmark & AI World Models Research","category":"Research","keywords":"machine learning AI 1024 1,024 embeddings sequence attention Python GitHub model evaluation","url":"research.html#algoverse","text":"Algoverse · international student research Looking for structure in a sequence. Sequence-Modelling Benchmark & AI World Models Research ········ ········ ········ ········ 1,024 dimensions 01 / data Understand the inputs. Work with sequential datasets containing image-frame representations, 1,024-dimensional embeddings and x-y position targets . Inspect array shapes, data types, distributions and missing values before training. 02 / models Question the choices. Investigate attention-based and sequence-model architectures , compare modelling decisions and document technical findings clearly. 03 / evaluation Make it reproducible. Use Python, Git and GitHub for reproducible experiments and collaborative workflows. Focus on a stronger benchmark rather than treating one model result as the conclusion. Sequence modelling Attention Data validation Model evaluation View my GitHub profile ↗"},{"title":"Causal Economics Replication & Policy Brief","category":"Research","keywords":"causality econometrics regression economics EE","url":"research.html#causal-economics","text":"Planned strand Causal Economics Replication & Policy Brief Replicate an empirical economics result and distinguish correlation from causation through research design, regression and causal-inference reasoning. The learning behind it ↗"},{"title":"Offline Economics RAG Evaluation Benchmark","category":"Research","keywords":"local AI retrieval faithfulness sources Ollama ChromaDB","url":"research.html#economics-rag-benchmark","text":"Planned strand Offline Economics RAG Evaluation Benchmark Turn my local economics assistant into a benchmarked research system by testing retrieval quality, answer faithfulness and source attribution . See the existing build ↗"},{"title":"Game Theory of Supplier Negotiation","category":"Research","keywords":"bargaining repeated games airline Singapore Melbourne EE economics Nash","url":"research.html#game-theory-research","text":"Planned strand Game Theory of Supplier Negotiation Apply strategic interaction, bargaining and repeated games to supplier decisions. Connect this with my Economics EE interest in Singapore–Melbourne airline competition. The learning behind it ↗"},{"title":"Projects","category":"Pages","keywords":"Projects","text":"FraudCrew, Hack the Future, local offline RAG assistants, controlled security lab and Solivia website.","url":"projects.html"},{"title":"FraudCrew: Agentic AI Fraud Evaluation","category":"Projects","keywords":"hackathon NTT DATA NUS Microsoft first place 60 alerts recall precision 20 hours","url":"projects.html#fraudcrew","text":"1st place · Hack the Future July 2026 FraudCrew Agentic AI Fraud Evaluation Developed and tested an evaluation workflow for a multi-agent fraud investigation system using Python and GitHub, as part of the NTT DATA / NUS / Microsoft hackathon project. Added red-team test scenarios and submitted work through a feature branch and pull request . My focus was on whether the system escalated the right cases, not just whether its output sounded convincing. View the first-place certificate ↗ the evaluation set 60 alerts evaluated 6 confirmed fraud cases 100% fraud recall 30% escalation precision Portfolio-reported test set. These results are not a claim about production performance. How the metrics fit together + The system escalated 20 alerts and cleared 40 . Given 6 confirmed fraud cases and 100% recall, the implied counts are 6 true positives, 14 false positives, 40 true negatives and 0 false negatives . Recall = 6 ÷ (6 + 0) = 100% Precision = 6 ÷ (6 + 14) = 30% The reduction from reviewing all 54 non-fraud alerts to reviewing 14 is 40 ÷ 54 = 74.1% . This is a false-positive review-volume comparison, not a general productivity guarantee. The previous portfolio also reported approximately 20 analyst hours saved . That remains a time-saving estimate: a timing study and its assumptions were not included with the project material. What I learned Agent evaluation, confusion-matrix metrics, red-team testing and collaborative Git workflows. Next iteration Calibrate escalation thresholds, expand adversarial and non-fraud cases, and evaluate cost-sensitive trade-offs."},{"title":"Offline RAG Assistants: Economics & Tennis","category":"Projects","keywords":"Ollama ChromaDB local inference AI LLM retrieval offline","url":"projects.html#offline-rag","text":"independent AI build Knowledge, kept local. Offline RAG Assistants: Economics & Tennis Built two domain-specific assistants with Ollama and ChromaDB , combining local model inference with vector-based retrieval. Separate knowledge bases for economics and tennis allow answers to use retrieved context. Both systems are designed to run fully locally and offline , keeping source material and inference on-device. Ollama ChromaDB Local LLM inference RAG Next iteration + A fixed evaluation question set, formal retrieval benchmarks, chunking and embedding comparisons, and clearer source attribution. Explore the research direction ↗"},{"title":"Controlled Cybersecurity & Ethical Hacking Lab","category":"Projects","keywords":"defensive logging honeypots network sockets isolated educational sandbox security","url":"projects.html#security-lab","text":"controlled educational environment Build carefully. Observe closely. Cybersecurity & Ethical Hacking Lab Built an isolated educational environment combining honeypots, socket-based communication, monitoring and defensive logging. A deliberately vulnerable booking application served as a sandbox for observing insecure handling and the defensive signals generated during testing. The work is limited to controlled, authorised systems . Honeypots Network sockets Defensive logging Ethical testing The learning focus is understanding and recognising security weaknesses in a contained lab."},{"title":"Solivia Website: Product & Commercial Web Build","category":"Projects","keywords":"HTML CSS healthcare B2B website information architecture","url":"projects.html#solivia-website","text":"startup build A home for a young company. Solivia Website Designed and launched solivia.life for a Singapore healthcare startup. Structured brand, company and product information for distributors, hospitals, clinics and commercial stakeholders. Connected web design with product positioning, market research and business-development requirements . HTML/CSS Information architecture B2B healthcare Visit the Solivia website ↗"},{"title":"Solivia Passion Project: from website to decision support","category":"Projects","keywords":"commercial decision support pricing calculator procurement passion research","url":"projects.html#solivia-project-link","text":"the next layer From telling a story to testing a decision. Solivia Commercial Decision Support System The web build and the research project are different pieces of work. My passion project adds a planned data layer, quotation logic, commercial-risk view and procurement evaluation . The research page contains the full roadmap, the technical write-up and a working synthetic quotation calculator . Open the passion project ↗"},{"title":"Co-curriculars","category":"Pages","keywords":"Co-curriculars","text":"Ongoing programmes, online courses, certificates, competitions, accepted programmes and readings.","url":"co-curriculars.html"},{"title":"Hack Club","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Building & shipping ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-hack-club","text":"Building & shipping Ongoing Hack Club Hack Club Useful for Data science, practical programming, GitHub projects and turning an idea into a working product. My learning focus + Spend 30 to 35 hours hacking and shipping projects. My focus is on a small, usable build with clear documentation, rather than collecting another unfinished prototype. Project practice Explore my builds ↗"},{"title":"Google AI: Attention Mechanism","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Machine learning ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-google-attention","text":"Machine learning Ongoing Google AI Google AI: Attention Mechanism Useful for Algoverse sequence modelling, attention-based architectures, high-dimensional embeddings and understanding model behaviour. My learning focus + Strengthen my understanding of how attention assigns relevance across a sequence and how this affects a model’s predictions. Use this learning to make clearer modelling and evaluation decisions. Technical foundations Connect to Algoverse ↗"},{"title":"Harvard CS50x","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Computer science ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-harvard-cs50x","text":"Computer science Ongoing Harvard Harvard CS50x Useful for Computer Science HL, programming fundamentals, algorithmic reasoning, debugging, the CS IA and independent software projects. My learning focus + Build a more systematic approach to problem decomposition and implementation. Apply the foundations to cleaner, more reliable project code. Technical foundations Connect to my projects ↗"},{"title":"IBM Data Fluency Pathways","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Data literacy ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-ibm-data-fluency","text":"Data literacy Ongoing IBM IBM Data Fluency Pathways Useful for Exploratory data analysis, data quality, choosing appropriate visualisations and explaining evidence to non-technical stakeholders. My learning focus + Practise moving from a question to a dataset, an analysis and a clearly communicated finding. Apply the same discipline to Solivia’s commercial information. Technical foundations Explore the data design ↗"},{"title":"Econometrics: Methods and Applications: Erasmus University Rotterdam","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Econometrics ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-econometrics","text":"Econometrics Ongoing Erasmus University Rotterdam Econometrics: Methods and Applications Useful for Solivia Decision Intelligence Suite, Causal Economics Replication, Economics EE, Economics Global IA, LSE Economics/Data Science, Manchester Data Science & Economics and Columbia Economics–Statistics. My learning focus + Develop regression, model specification, endogeneity and time-series reasoning. My aim is a stronger empirical basis for economics research, with assumptions and limitations made explicit. Highest priority Connect to forecast evaluation ↗"},{"title":"Operations Research Specialization: National Taiwan University","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Optimisation ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-operations-research","text":"Optimisation Ongoing National Taiwan University Operations Research Specialization Useful for Solivia procurement optimisation, inventory decisions, constrained decision-making, Mathematics AA HL IA ideas and UNSW Data Science & Decisions. My learning focus + Work with objective functions, constraints and linear or integer decision models. Move beyond predicting an outcome to choosing a feasible action. Highest priority Explore the optimisation model ↗"},{"title":"A Crash Course in Causality: University of Pennsylvania","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Causal inference ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-causality","text":"Causal inference Ongoing University of Pennsylvania A Crash Course in Causality Useful for Causal Economics Replication & Policy Brief, Economics EE methodology, Economics Global IA, UCL Social Sciences with Data Science, Warwick PPE/Social Sciences and Columbia/NYU pathways. My learning focus + Distinguish correlation from causation through confounding, treatment effects, observational data and research design. Use these ideas to make research claims more defensible. Highest priority Explore the research strand ↗"},{"title":"Bayesian Statistics Specialization: UC Santa Cruz","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Statistics ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-bayesian-statistics","text":"Statistics Ongoing UC Santa Cruz Bayesian Statistics Specialization Useful for Algoverse evaluation, Solivia uncertainty modelling, Monte Carlo and Bayesian forecasting, Mathematics IA ideas and quantitative university applications. My learning focus + Develop probability, uncertainty estimation and probabilistic reasoning. Prioritise statistical depth over another generic machine-learning certificate. Very high priority Connect to uncertainty & risk ↗"},{"title":"Databases and SQL for Data Science with Python: IBM","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Data engineering ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-ibm-sql","text":"Data engineering Ongoing IBM Databases and SQL for Data Science with Python Useful for Solivia’s data layer, CS IA work, database design, data pipelines, GitHub projects, internships and data-science workflows. My learning focus + Build the SQL skills needed to organise linked commercial records, query them consistently and make the data layer easier to maintain. Core applied skill Explore the proposed SQL structure ↗"},{"title":"Game Theory: Stanford University & University of British Columbia","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Economics ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-game-theory","text":"Economics Ongoing Stanford University & University of British Columbia Game Theory Useful for Game Theory of Supplier Negotiation, my Singapore–Melbourne airline repeated-game EE, Economics HL, PPE/Economics applications and supplier bargaining or tender strategy. My learning focus + Study strategic interaction, Nash equilibrium, repeated games, Bayesian games and auctions. Connect the theory to airline competition and supplier negotiations. Core applied skill Connect to supplier negotiation ↗"},{"title":"Supply Chain Analytics Specialization: Rutgers University","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"Supply-chain analytics ongoing programme course online learning","url":"co-curriculars.html#course-supply-chain","text":"Supply-chain analytics Ongoing Rutgers University Supply Chain Analytics Specialization Useful for Solivia demand forecasting, inventory, procurement, sourcing, logistics, supplier risk and possible Business Management IA applications. My learning focus + Make the Solivia decision model more operationally realistic. Connect quantitative methods to the constraints of sourcing, delivery and working capital. Applied project depth Explore the project roadmap ↗"},{"title":"Hack the Future AI Hackathon certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"NTT DATA × Microsoft Award certificate 1st place completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-hack-the-future","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ 1st place 2026 NTT DATA × Microsoft Hack the Future AI Hackathon Useful for Agentic AI, model evaluation, collaborative coding and communicating a technical solution. About this evidence + First-place award for the Hack the Future AI Hackathon 2026. My related FraudCrew project explores evaluation and escalation decisions. See the project & metrics ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Building AI Agents with Microsoft Copilot Studio certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"James Cook Institute Course Participation certificate completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-agentic-ai","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation certificate 8 April 2026 James Cook Institute Building AI Agents with Microsoft Copilot Studio Useful for Agent workflows, low-code AI prototyping and understanding where evaluation and human oversight fit. About this evidence + The uploaded document is headed “Certificate of Participation”. This is the evidence attached to my Agentic AI course entry. Connect to agent evaluation ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Future Leaders Lab certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Westbourne College Singapore Entrepreneurship Participation confirmed completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-future-leaders","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation confirmed 8 to 12 June 2026 Westbourne College Singapore Future Leaders Lab Useful for Entrepreneurship, business problem-solving, commercial thinking and communicating ideas. About this evidence + Business entrepreneurship programme organised by Westbourne College and instructed by INSEAD Adjunct Professor Adrian Johnson. Participation letter issued on 17 August 2026. Connect to startup experience ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Girls Who Code: Intro to Data Science certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Girls Who Code Course Completed completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-girls-who-code","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Completed Girls Who Code Girls Who Code: Intro to Data Science Useful for Data-science foundations, analytical thinking and applying mathematics to data. About this evidence + The attached certificate confirms completion of Intro to Data Science. It is kept separate from my accepted Python Summer Programme entry, which is not evidence of full programme completion. See the separate summer entry ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"National Economics & Financial Management Challenge certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"NUS Economics Society Competition Participation certificate completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-nusefmc","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation certificate 2026 NUS Economics Society National Economics & Financial Management Challenge Useful for Economics, financial reasoning, problem-solving and teamwork. About this evidence + NUSEFMC / NEFMC participation with Team IB45, representing Westbourne College Singapore. The certificate recognises economics and problem-solving skills. Connect to Economics HL ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"YOUNNOVATE: AI for Social Good certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Y:WAIT Hackathon Participation certificate completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-younnovate","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation certificate 2026 Y:WAIT YOUNNOVATE: AI for Social Good Useful for Data science, social awareness, entrepreneurial thinking and pitching technology for real-world problems. About this evidence + A hackathon organised by Young Women in AI & Technology, connecting technopreneurial solutions with the UN Sustainable Development Goals. The certificate and pitch evidence are grouped here. Explore related builds ↗ View the pitch evidence ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Harvard Crimson Global Case Competition certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Harvard Crimson Global Case Competition Case competition Participation certificate completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-harvard-crimson","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation certificate 2025 Harvard Crimson Global Case Competition Harvard Crimson Global Case Competition Useful for Case analysis, structured business reasoning, teamwork and presenting recommendations. About this evidence + Participation evidence for the 2025 competition. The learning connection is to analysing a business problem and explaining a reasoned recommendation. Connect to commercial research ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Youth Circles: Intercultural Leadership certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Youth Circles Leadership Participation certificate completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-youth-circles","text":"Take a closer look ⤢ Participation certificate Youth Circles Youth Circles: Intercultural Leadership Useful for Intercultural collaboration, facilitation, public speaking and stakeholder communication. About this evidence + Participation in Youth Circles, supported by the UNESCO Intercultural Leadership Programme. My related partnership-lead experience sits on the Experience page. Explore the experience ↗ Open original certificate ↗"},{"title":"Wharton Global High School Investment Competition certificate","category":"Certificates","keywords":"Wharton Global Youth Program Investment competition Participation credential completed evidence","url":"co-curriculars.html#cert-wharton","text":"✧ WHARTON GLOBAL YOUTH PROGRAM Investment Competition 2025 to 2026 · Participation credential Participation credential 2025 to 2026 Wharton Global Youth Program Wharton Global High School Investment Competition Useful for Strategy-building, risk, diversification, company and industry analysis, teamwork and financial communication. About this evidence + The supplied digital participation credential records the competition and issuer. It links to the issuer-hosted credential page; it is not a winner or placement claim. Connect to economics & data ↗ Open credential page ↗ Download credential JSON ↗"},{"title":"Girls Who Code Python Summer Programme: Accepted","category":"Co-curriculars","keywords":"June 29 2026 August 14 online mathematics data science Morgan Stanley accepted summer","url":"co-curriculars.html#girls-who-code-summer","text":"✉ Accepted 29 June to 14 August 2026 · Online Girls Who Code Python Summer Programme Useful for: Python practice, data science, mathematical reasoning and confidence in independent technical work. The recorded status for this programme is accepted . The separate Intro to Data Science certificate above does not establish completion of the whole summer programme."},{"title":"Forecasting: Principles and Practice, forecast accuracy reference","category":"Readings","keywords":"Hyndman Athanasopoulos MAE RMSE time series training test","url":"co-curriculars.html#reading-forecasting","text":"Method reference · not logged as read Forecasting: Principles and Practice Rob J Hyndman & George Athanasopoulos · Third edition Project connection: point-forecast evaluation and separating training observations from later test observations. See the project’s evaluation design ↗ Open the reference ↗"},{"title":"Principles of Economics: marginal revenue reference","category":"Readings","keywords":"OpenStax MR MC marginal revenue marginal cost pricing","url":"co-curriculars.html#reading-marginal-revenue","text":"Method reference · not logged as read Principles of Economics OpenStax · Third edition Project connection: defining marginal revenue and distinguishing the value of additional sales from their selling price. See the marginal-revenue example ↗ Open the reference ↗"},{"title":"Experience","category":"Pages","keywords":"Experience","text":"B9 Dental Clementi internship, Pinterest TikTok Snapchat job shadowing, Solivia and Youth Circles.","url":"experience.html"},{"title":"B9 Dental Clementi: Data Operations Analyst (Digitisation & Privacy)","category":"Experience","keywords":"internship digitization records confidential privacy accuracy data operations analyst 2026","url":"experience.html#b9-dental","text":"2026 One-week internship Internship Clementi, Singapore B9 Dental Data Operations Analyst (Digitisation & Privacy) Digitised, scanned and systematically organised confidential records , using consistent naming and indexing to improve retrievability. Handled sensitive information with attention to privacy, accuracy and information governance . This experience showed me how reliable data operations depend on disciplined processes, not only software. Digitisation Data accuracy Privacy Records governance What stayed with me Good analysis begins before modelling, with information that is organised, accurate and handled responsibly."},{"title":"Pinterest, TikTok & Snapchat: Professional Job Shadowing","category":"Experience","keywords":"data engineering commercial analytics science job shadowing product system design reliability","url":"experience.html#job-shadowing","text":"Professional exposure Job shadowing Job shadowing Pinterest · TikTok · Snapchat Across data, engineering and commercial professionals Data & analysis Observed how business questions become analysis, metrics and recommendations , and how modelling choices connect to product goals. Engineering Learned about system design, debugging, testing and reliability trade-offs in professional workflows. Commercial thinking Explored how technical capabilities connect to customer needs and measurable business value . The connection Saw why effective technical work needs both a sound method and a clear understanding of the decision it supports. Data analytics Data science Engineering Commercial"},{"title":"Solivia Pte. Ltd.: Co-Founder","category":"Experience","keywords":"startup healthcare B2B cofounder market research financial management tender procurement pricing","url":"experience.html#solivia-experience","text":"Current Startup leadership Co-Founder Solivia Pte. Ltd. Singapore · Healthcare and medical-device startup Help build the operating foundation of a B2B startup serving healthcare distributors, hospitals and clinics . Conduct market sizing, competitor and pricing research, procurement and tender analysis across Southeast Asia and Australia/New Zealand. Support financial management, cost modelling, budgeting and commercial scenario planning. Market research Pricing Financial management B2B healthcare The passion project this inspired ↗ The website I built ↗"},{"title":"Youth Circles: Partnership Lead","category":"Experience","keywords":"UNESCO leadership intercultural facilitation public speaking partnership","url":"experience.html#youth-circles-experience","text":"2025 to present Leadership & people Partnership Lead Youth Circles UNESCO-supported Intercultural Leadership Programme Support intercultural leadership activities, youth discussions and collaborative programmes. Develop public speaking, facilitation, stakeholder communication and leadership through programme participation and committee work. Intercultural collaboration Communication Facilitation View the participation certificate ↗"},{"title":"Education","category":"Pages","keywords":"Education","text":"Westbourne College Singapore, IB Diploma Programme, Mathematics AA, Economics and Computer Science HL.","url":"education.html"},{"title":"International Baccalaureate Diploma Programme: Westbourne College Singapore","category":"Education","keywords":"IBDP 2025 2027 May HL SL mathematics economics computer science business English Hindi","url":"education.html#ib-diploma","text":"2025 to 2027 Expected completion: May 2027 International Baccalaureate Diploma Programme Westbourne College Singapore Higher Level 01 Mathematics AA 02 Economics 03 Computer Science Standard Level 01 Business Management 02 English Language & Literature 03 Hindi"},{"title":"Quantitative & Interdisciplinary Academic Focus","category":"Education","keywords":"statistics econometrics game theory decision science optimisation responsible AI","url":"education.html#academic-focus","text":"how the pieces connect A toolkit, not separate boxes. mathematics Represent uncertainty. Use probability, statistics and mathematical reasoning to make assumptions explicit and evaluate what a model can support. See the quantitative write-up ↗ economics Understand decisions. Connect incentives, game theory and commercial trade-offs with questions about pricing, procurement and competition. Explore the research notebook ↗ computer science Make it testable. Apply computational problem-solving through machine-learning experiments, local AI systems and usable interfaces. Explore practical projects ↗ My academic interests include statistics, econometrics, game theory, decision science, optimisation and responsible AI . I apply classroom ideas through machine-learning research, local AI systems and Solivia work."},{"title":"Skills","category":"Pages","keywords":"Skills","text":"Python NumPy Git GitHub HTML CSS data analysis, AI, commercial research and communication.","url":"skills.html"},{"title":"Technical Skills: Python, NumPy, Git, RAG & Web Development","category":"Skills","keywords":"data validation exploratory analysis machine learning attention GitHub Ollama ChromaDB LLM HTML CSS sockets cybersecurity","url":"skills.html#technical-skills","text":"⌘ used across projects & research Technical Data & experimentation Python, NumPy, data validation, exploratory data analysis and machine-learning evaluation. Research exposure to attention mechanisms and sequence data. Build & collaborate Git/GitHub, HTML/CSS, Ollama, ChromaDB, RAG and local LLM inference. Socket-based programming and controlled cybersecurity-lab fundamentals. See these tools in context ↗"},{"title":"Business & Research Skills","category":"Skills","keywords":"market research competitor pricing cost modelling budgeting tender supplier logistics commercial risk writing","url":"skills.html#business-research-skills","text":"✧ practised through startup work Business & research Understand the context Market research, competitor analysis, pricing research, tender research and commercial analysis. Explain the decision Cost and margin modelling, supplier and logistics cost analysis, budgeting and research writing. Connect numerical work to a clear business question. See the startup experience ↗"},{"title":"Communication & Leadership Skills","category":"Skills","keywords":"public speaking debate presentations stakeholder teamwork intercultural facilitation","url":"skills.html#communication-skills","text":"✿ practised with people Communication & leadership Make ideas understandable Public speaking, debate, presentation and stakeholder communication. Work across perspectives Teamwork, intercultural collaboration, professional observation and facilitation. Learn by listening, explaining and building with others. See the leadership experience ↗"},{"title":"Skills in Development: SQL, Econometrics, Optimisation & Forecasting","category":"Skills","keywords":"Bayesian statistics causal inference game theory inventory procurement demand forecasting learning","url":"skills.html#developing-skills","text":"deliberately developing The next layer of depth. These are the methods I’m strengthening through ongoing coursework and planned research, not a claim that every method has already been implemented. SQL & relational data Econometrics Causal inference Bayesian statistics Demand forecasting Inventory planning Linear/integer optimisation Game theory Supply-chain analytics Explore the learning plan ↗ See where the methods will be used ↗"},{"title":"Solivia passion project","category":"Pages","keywords":"Solivia passion project","text":"Commercial Decision Support System, technical case study, pricing, MR, statistics, procurement, calculator and roadmap.","url":"passion-project.html"},{"title":"Solivia passion project: overview, phases and estimated hours","category":"Passion project","keywords":"decision intelligence suite stats statistics mathematics MR pricing procurement 20 steps","url":"passion-project.html#overview","text":"3 planned phases 20 defined steps 98 to 157 estimated project hours These totals are calculated from the supplied plan: 36 to 56 hours for Phase 1, 50 to 80 for Phase 2 and 12 to 21 for Phase 3. They are estimates, not logged work."},{"title":"Solivia business problem: pricing, quotations and procurement","category":"Passion project","keywords":"business research healthcare distributor decision support","url":"passion-project.html#business-problem","text":"01 / explain the business problem A good quote is more than a selling price. My central question is how a healthcare distribution startup can make more defensible pricing, quotation and procurement decisions when supplier costs, freight, demand and delivery timing are uncertain. The proposed users are the people preparing quotations, comparing procurement alternatives and reviewing commercial opportunities. The system should make the cost basis, expected contribution, cash requirement and uncertainty visible beside each decision. Scope boundary: this is a commercial decision-support project. It does not make clinical recommendations or use patient records."},{"title":"Solivia existing workflow and evidence collection","category":"Passion project","keywords":"stakeholders suppliers quotations enquiries purchasing existing process","url":"passion-project.html#existing-workflow","text":"02 / describe the existing workflow Map the work before claiming to improve it. The first task is to document how supplier information, quotations, enquiries and purchasing decisions currently move through Solivia. I will use stakeholder input and available records to identify genuine difficulties. Supplier information → Quotation → Enquiry / order → Procurement & delivery No baseline workflow study is attached to the plan. I therefore do not assume a particular spreadsheet setup, invent delays or assign an efficiency gain before measuring the existing process."},{"title":"Solivia data design: SQL schema, audit statistics and provenance","category":"Passion project","keywords":"database products suppliers orders sample size completeness duplicates missing data SQL statistics","url":"passion-project.html#data-design","text":"03 / document the data design Give every number a source. The plan calls for linked SQL records covering products, suppliers, prices, minimum order quantities, lead times, quotations and enquiries. A proposed relational structure could group the information as follows; this is a design sketch, not a deployed database . Products & supplier offers Product IDs, supplier IDs, units, quantity tiers, currencies and validity dates. Distributors & opportunities Enquiry IDs, requested quantities, stages, next actions and estimated values. Quotations & quote lines Linked products, quantity, delivery basis, cost components and assumptions. Orders & deliveries Confirmed quantities, dated events, lead times and fulfilment records. Stable identifiers should link these records. Transaction lines should be separated from document headers, and units, currencies, source references and dates retained rather than inferred later. Observed Supported by an actual quotation, order, record or documented event. Estimated A stated assumption or range, such as an unverified conversion probability. Synthetic A constructed example used to demonstrate or stress-test a method. What the data audit will quantify Coverage Number of unique products, suppliers and completed orders; first and last usable dates; missing periods by product. Completeness Required populated fields ÷ required field slots × 100%. A missing sale is not automatically a zero sale. Integrity Duplicate primary keys, unmatched references, inconsistent units and records lacking a source or date. Lead-time statistics Count, mean, median and spread of observed lead times, with the event definition and units stated. These are proposed audit measures. The plan supplies no production row counts or observed summary statistics, so none are invented here."},{"title":"Solivia quantitative methodology: cost, margin, statistics and MR","category":"Passion project","keywords":"math mathematics mathematical reasoning formulas quantification contribution margin landed cost","url":"passion-project.html#methodology","text":"04 / explain the decision methodology Make the reasoning inspectable. The following equations turn the plan into a proposed quantitative specification. They are methodological additions , not evidence that the full model has been built. The browser lab implements the simplified quotation equations only. A. Landed cost, quotation and contribution For an order of q units, let c be the supplier cost per unit, x the exchange rate into SGD, F total freight and I total insurance. In the simplified demonstration: Total included cost, in SGD C(q) = q × c × x + F + I Landed cost per unit c L = C(q) / q Required price for target margin m P* = c L / (1 − m) Contribution and contribution margin CM = qP − C(q) Margin = CM / (qP) Why divide by (1 − m)? Because margin is measured against revenue: m = (P − c L ) / P . Rearranging gives P(1 − m) = c L . Multiplying cost by 1 + m would instead apply a markup. The lab rounds the required price up to the nearest cent to preserve the selected minimum margin. It uses that quoted price for revenue and contribution, with full-precision costs underneath. Model boundary: quantity is positive and the target margin is below 100%. All units are assumed sold. Only separately payable freight and insurance should be entered, so delivery charges already included in a supplier price are not counted twice. Other variable costs, taxes, overhead and payment timing are not modelled in the public example. A live quotation would need those inputs checked. B. MR: does selling more add enough value? Marginal revenue (MR) is the change in total revenue per additional unit sold. For two discrete commercial scenarios, compare changes in total revenue and total relevant cost, not just the new price. [2] MR = (R₂ − R₁) / (q₂ − q₁) MC = (C₂ − C₁) / (q₂ − q₁) ΔCM = (R₂ − C₂) − (R₁ − C₁) Synthetic scenario A 1,000 units at S$20 Total revenue: S$20,000 Total relevant cost: S$12,500 Contribution: S$7,500 Synthetic scenario B 1,200 units at S$19 Total revenue: S$22,800 Total relevant cost: S$14,400 Contribution: S$8,400 In this constructed comparison, MR = S$2,800 / 200 = S$14 per additional unit, MC = S$1,900 / 200 = S$9.50 , and contribution rises by S$900 . MR is below the new S$19 price because the lower price applies to every unit in scenario B. Quantities, prices and costs are assumptions, not Solivia observations. This is a finite scenario comparison, not an estimated demand curve or proof that extra demand exists. A continuous model could use R(q) = qP(q) and MR = dR/dq, but that would require a justified demand specification. C. Statistics: evaluate later observations The source plan gates forecasting on data readiness. Where evidence is sufficient, compare simple baselines with a justified alternative using later observations not used to fit the model . For error e t = y t − ŷ t : [3] MAE = Σ|e t | / n RMSE = √(Σe t ² / n) Bias = Σe t / n MAE expresses average absolute error in the original units. RMSE gives large misses more influence. Positive bias under this sign convention means underprediction. Model choices should be made on training/validation data, with a final test reserved for evaluation. Synthetic illustration only Six periods. Two illustrative forecasts. Baseline Candidate Constructed demand Selected forecast Synthetic demand and forecast across six periods Constructed demand: 100, 120, 110, 140, 130, 150 units. Baseline forecast: 110, 110, 120, 120, 140, 130 units. MAE 13.33 units RMSE 14.14 units Bias +3.33 units See every number behind the illustration + All six rows are synthetic, not a Solivia sales history. Period Constructed demand Baseline Candidate 1 100 110 105 2 120 110 118 3 110 120 115 4 140 120 132 5 130 140 134 6 150 130 143 For the baseline, absolute errors sum to 80 and squared errors sum to 1,200. Therefore MAE = 80/6 = 13.33 and RMSE = √(1,200/6) = 14.14 . For the candidate, MAE = 31/6 = 5.17 , RMSE = √(183/6) = 5.52 and bias = 0.50 . These calculations illustrate the measures. They do not establish the quality of a trained model or a realised improvement. D. Inventory and commercial risk A proposed inventory rule links the reorder point to demand over the replenishment lead time. In a simplified deterministic illustration: ROP = Expected lead-time demand + Safety stock Assume 40 units per day , a 7-day lead time and an explicitly assumed 60-unit buffer . The illustrative reorder point is 40 × 7 + 60 = 340 units . The buffer is a scenario input, not an estimated service-level guarantee. In the full project, safety stock should be justified by observed demand and lead-time uncertainty where possible. When the observations are insufficient, use labelled ranges rather than presenting chosen buffers as statistically estimated. Sales concentration Distributor share = that distributor’s confirmed sales ÷ total confirmed sales. Report pipeline shares separately; a zero denominator means the sales share is undefined. Concentration index HHI = Σs i ², using shares as fractions between 0 and 1. This proposed indicator summarises concentration; no risk thresholds are asserted. Weighted pipeline Σ(probability × opportunity value). Every conversion probability is an assumption unless supported by evidence. This is not booked revenue. Peak cash exposure Maximum cumulative cash shortfall over the stated timeline. It requires payment dates and terms, not just a contribution-margin calculation. E. Optimisation: choose a feasible order The plan proposes constrained procurement optimisation. One possible single-period deterministic model is shown below, with zero starting inventory and fixed scenario prices and demand. It is a proposed specification, not an implemented solver. For product i , let q i be ordered units, s i units sold and y i a binary order indicator. Let p be selling price, c unit purchase cost, h additional holding cost per unsold unit, and k a fixed order cost. Maximise scenario contribution after relevant inventory costs max Σ[p i s i − c i q i − h i (q i − s i ) − k i y i ] Subject to supported constraints 0 ≤ s i ≤ d i , s i ≤ q i MOQ i y i ≤ q i ≤ U i y i Σ(c i q i + k i y i ) ≤ B Σv i q i ≤ V q i , s i are non-negative integers; y i ∈ {0, 1} Here d is scenario demand, U a valid order upper bound, B the cash budget, v storage per unit and V total storage capacity. Purchase costs already cover all ordered units; h adds holding cost rather than charging for unsold units a second time. Under optional purchasing, the zero-order solution is feasible. A “no feasible solution” test only makes sense once supported mandatory commitments or other incompatible constraints are introduced. The application should explain such conflicts rather than output an impossible order. The full evaluation would compare the optimiser with simpler ordering rules under the same scenarios , examining contribution, stockouts, excess inventory and cash exposure. A stochastic extension or Monte Carlo stress test would require explicit probability assumptions."},{"title":"Landed cost and contribution margin formulas","category":"Passion project","keywords":"quotation price markup freight insurance exchange rate FX costs calculator","url":"passion-project.html#cost-margin","text":"A. Landed cost, quotation and contribution For an order of q units, let c be the supplier cost per unit, x the exchange rate into SGD, F total freight and I total insurance. In the simplified demonstration: Total included cost, in SGD C(q) = q × c × x + F + I Landed cost per unit c L = C(q) / q Required price for target margin m P* = c L / (1 − m) Contribution and contribution margin CM = qP − C(q) Margin = CM / (qP) Why divide by (1 − m)? Because margin is measured against revenue: m = (P − c L ) / P . Rearranging gives P(1 − m) = c L . Multiplying cost by 1 + m would instead apply a markup. The lab rounds the required price up to the nearest cent to preserve the selected minimum margin. It uses that quoted price for revenue and contribution, with full-precision costs underneath. Model boundary: quantity is positive and the target margin is below 100%. All units are assumed sold. Only separately payable freight and insurance should be entered, so delivery charges already included in a supplier price are not counted twice. Other variable costs, taxes, overhead and payment timing are not modelled in the public example. A live quotation would need those inputs checked."},{"title":"MR: marginal revenue and incremental contribution","category":"Passion project","keywords":"MR MC marginal revenue marginal cost mathematical reasoning pricing discounts economics","url":"passion-project.html#marginal-revenue","text":"B. MR: does selling more add enough value? Marginal revenue (MR) is the change in total revenue per additional unit sold. For two discrete commercial scenarios, compare changes in total revenue and total relevant cost, not just the new price. [2] MR = (R₂ − R₁) / (q₂ − q₁) MC = (C₂ − C₁) / (q₂ − q₁) ΔCM = (R₂ − C₂) − (R₁ − C₁) Synthetic scenario A 1,000 units at S$20 Total revenue: S$20,000 Total relevant cost: S$12,500 Contribution: S$7,500 Synthetic scenario B 1,200 units at S$19 Total revenue: S$22,800 Total relevant cost: S$14,400 Contribution: S$8,400 In this constructed comparison, MR = S$2,800 / 200 = S$14 per additional unit, MC = S$1,900 / 200 = S$9.50 , and contribution rises by S$900 . MR is below the new S$19 price because the lower price applies to every unit in scenario B. Quantities, prices and costs are assumptions, not Solivia observations. This is a finite scenario comparison, not an estimated demand curve or proof that extra demand exists. A continuous model could use R(q) = qP(q) and MR = dR/dq, but that would require a justified demand specification."},{"title":"Forecast evaluation: MAE, RMSE, bias and synthetic demand data","category":"Passion project","keywords":"statistics stats time series holdout demand forecasting baseline errors Python quantitative","url":"passion-project.html#forecasting","text":"C. Statistics: evaluate later observations The source plan gates forecasting on data readiness. Where evidence is sufficient, compare simple baselines with a justified alternative using later observations not used to fit the model . For error e t = y t − ŷ t : [3] MAE = Σ|e t | / n RMSE = √(Σe t ² / n) Bias = Σe t / n MAE expresses average absolute error in the original units. RMSE gives large misses more influence. Positive bias under this sign convention means underprediction. Model choices should be made on training/validation data, with a final test reserved for evaluation. Synthetic illustration only Six periods. Two illustrative forecasts. Baseline Candidate Constructed demand Selected forecast Synthetic demand and forecast across six periods Constructed demand: 100, 120, 110, 140, 130, 150 units. Baseline forecast: 110, 110, 120, 120, 140, 130 units. MAE 13.33 units RMSE 14.14 units Bias +3.33 units See every number behind the illustration + All six rows are synthetic, not a Solivia sales history. Period Constructed demand Baseline Candidate 1 100 110 105 2 120 110 118 3 110 120 115 4 140 120 132 5 130 140 134 6 150 130 143 For the baseline, absolute errors sum to 80 and squared errors sum to 1,200. Therefore MAE = 80/6 = 13.33 and RMSE = √(1,200/6) = 14.14 . For the candidate, MAE = 31/6 = 5.17 , RMSE = √(183/6) = 5.52 and bias = 0.50 . These calculations illustrate the measures. They do not establish the quality of a trained model or a realised improvement."},{"title":"Inventory rules, uncertainty and commercial risk metrics","category":"Passion project","keywords":"reorder point safety stock ROP supplier distributor concentration HHI working capital risk probability","url":"passion-project.html#inventory-risk","text":"D. Inventory and commercial risk A proposed inventory rule links the reorder point to demand over the replenishment lead time. In a simplified deterministic illustration: ROP = Expected lead-time demand + Safety stock Assume 40 units per day , a 7-day lead time and an explicitly assumed 60-unit buffer . The illustrative reorder point is 40 × 7 + 60 = 340 units . The buffer is a scenario input, not an estimated service-level guarantee. In the full project, safety stock should be justified by observed demand and lead-time uncertainty where possible. When the observations are insufficient, use labelled ranges rather than presenting chosen buffers as statistically estimated. Sales concentration Distributor share = that distributor’s confirmed sales ÷ total confirmed sales. Report pipeline shares separately; a zero denominator means the sales share is undefined. Concentration index HHI = Σs i ², using shares as fractions between 0 and 1. This proposed indicator summarises concentration; no risk thresholds are asserted. Weighted pipeline Σ(probability × opportunity value). Every conversion probability is an assumption unless supported by evidence. This is not booked revenue. Peak cash exposure Maximum cumulative cash shortfall over the stated timeline. It requires payment dates and terms, not just a contribution-margin calculation."},{"title":"Constrained procurement optimisation: objective and decision variables","category":"Passion project","keywords":"operations research OR linear integer optimisation budget MOQ storage cash contribution constraints","url":"passion-project.html#optimisation","text":"E. Optimisation: choose a feasible order The plan proposes constrained procurement optimisation. One possible single-period deterministic model is shown below, with zero starting inventory and fixed scenario prices and demand. It is a proposed specification, not an implemented solver. For product i , let q i be ordered units, s i units sold and y i a binary order indicator. Let p be selling price, c unit purchase cost, h additional holding cost per unsold unit, and k a fixed order cost. Maximise scenario contribution after relevant inventory costs max Σ[p i s i − c i q i − h i (q i − s i ) − k i y i ] Subject to supported constraints 0 ≤ s i ≤ d i , s i ≤ q i MOQ i y i ≤ q i ≤ U i y i Σ(c i q i + k i y i ) ≤ B Σv i q i ≤ V q i , s i are non-negative integers; y i ∈ {0, 1} Here d is scenario demand, U a valid order upper bound, B the cash budget, v storage per unit and V total storage capacity. Purchase costs already cover all ordered units; h adds holding cost rather than charging for unsold units a second time. Under optional purchasing, the zero-order solution is feasible. A “no feasible solution” test only makes sense once supported mandatory commitments or other incompatible constraints are introduced. The application should explain such conflicts rather than output an impossible order. The full evaluation would compare the optimiser with simpler ordering rules under the same scenarios , examining contribution, stockouts, excess inventory and cash exposure. A stochastic extension or Monte Carlo stress test would require explicit probability assumptions."},{"title":"Solivia system design and architecture: private data and public demonstration","category":"Passion project","keywords":"architecture SQL calculation engine interface privacy validation public synthetic","url":"passion-project.html#system-design","text":"05 / describe the system design Keep the logic connected. Keep private data private. 01 Private records Linked SQL tables, provenance and controlled access. 02 Decision logic Validated costs, assumptions, scenarios and supported constraints. 03 Working interface Quotation inputs, alternatives, warnings and an explanation. The architecture above is the intended commercial workflow. The public portfolio runs separately : its quotation lab uses only the inputs shown below, and its forecast chart uses fixed synthetic arrays. Neither connects to company systems. Implemented in this website Cross-page search, the simplified quotation calculator, input validation and a synthetic forecast-error visualisation. Planned for the full project The private relational database, stakeholder-validated quotation workflow, forecasting where data permits, and a procurement optimiser."},{"title":"Interactive Solivia quotation scenario lab: synthetic price and margin calculator","category":"Passion project","keywords":"calculator synthetic statistics math margin cash budget exchange rate freight insurance quantity","url":"passion-project.html#scenario-lab","text":"06 / demonstrate a complete commercial scenario A small lab for “what changes when…?” Change the assumptions to see the arithmetic update. This is a synthetic educational scenario , not a live Solivia quotation or a forecast of demand. Synthetic inputs · calculated outputs Reset inputs ↺ Order quantity Units Supplier cost per unit Supplier-currency units Exchange rate SGD per supplier-currency unit Freight per order SGD Insurance per order SGD Target contribution margin Percent of revenue Available cash budget SGD Minimum quoted unit price S$16.67 Rounded up to the nearest cent Landed unit cost S$12.50 Total included cost S$12,500.00 Illustrative revenue S$16,670.00 Contribution S$4,170.00 Actual quoted margin 25.01% Cash budget headroom S$2,500.00 Procurement Freight Insurance Contribution The included upfront cost fits within the illustrative cash budget. All included costs are assumed payable upfront; no customer deposit is assumed. Cash headroom is therefore budget minus included cost. This is not a full working-capital timeline. Other costs are excluded, not silently estimated. Read the default scenario from start to finish Inputs: 1,000 units, supplier cost 12 per unit, exchange rate 1.00, freight S$400, insurance S$100 and target margin 25%. Calculation: C = 1,000 × 12 × 1 + 400 + 100 = S$12,500 . Cost per unit is S$12.50. Required unrounded price is 12.50 / 0.75 = S$16.666…; the rounded-up quotation is S$16.67 . Output: revenue S$16,670, contribution S$4,170 and actual quoted margin 25.015% before display rounding. With a S$15,000 budget, the included upfront cost leaves S$2,500 of headroom. Sensitivity: increase freight by S$200 while leaving all other inputs unchanged. Total included cost becomes S$12,700 and the new minimum price is S$16.94 . At the original S$16.67 price, contribution would instead fall by S$200 and margin to about 23.82% . An input assumption changes the required quote. All business inputs in this walkthrough are constructed for demonstration. The dynamic lab may show different values after editing; this walkthrough always describes the stated defaults."},{"title":"Solivia evaluation plan: calculation accuracy, forecast errors and measured business value","category":"Passion project","keywords":"stats measurement baseline task time testing stockouts cash savings results uncertainty","url":"passion-project.html#evaluation","text":"07 / evaluate results and business value Measure what changed. Do not guess the benefit. No measured commercial outcomes are provided in the source plan. The completed case study should report what testing actually establishes, with the denominator, test set and assumptions visible. 01 Calculation checks Pass rate = passed cases / tested cases. Compare the engine with hand calculations, boundary cases, missing inputs and supplier-tier transitions. State the rounding rule and tolerance. 02 Forecast evaluation Report MAE, RMSE and bias on later observations. Include sample size, forecast horizon and baseline performance. Defer claims where the series cannot support evaluation. 03 Decision quality Compare contribution, stockouts, excess inventory and cash exposure under identical scenarios. Keep simulated improvements distinct from observed outcomes. 04 Workflow evidence Record stakeholder feedback and, where tested, comparable task times. Time change = (baseline − system time) / baseline × 100% , with sample size and task conditions stated. Current evidence status: the plan defines intended work. Website demonstrations illustrate calculations. Neither establishes increased revenue, realised cost savings, a production forecast or validated operational improvements."},{"title":"Solivia project limitations, assumptions and reproducibility","category":"Passion project","keywords":"missing data confidentiality model limitations simulation not operational evidence","url":"passion-project.html#limitations","text":"08 / discuss limitations and finish the report The limits belong beside the result. Thin sales history, missing dates, inconsistent product definitions and uncertain lead times may limit forecasting. An optimiser can only choose within its stated objective and constraints; a numerically attractive order is not useful when important business restrictions are absent. The public examples omit taxes, other variable costs, credit terms, overhead and interactions between products. A synthetic dataset can demonstrate logic, but cannot validate Solivia’s actual demand or commercial performance . The final report should state what is operational versus experimental, document assumptions and sources, show selected anonymised workflows, and link to reproducible code only when the relevant repository is ready. Until then, this site links to my GitHub profile , not an invented project repository. My GitHub profile ↗ Methods I’m learning ↗"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 1: Create the product and supplier database","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-1","text":"1.1 Create the product and supplier database + Inventory the information Solivia actually possesses, then organise products, suppliers, prices, minimum order quantities, lead times, quotations and enquiries into linked SQL tables. Record units, currencies, sources and dates. Separate observed information from estimates, and create a sanitised dataset for the public demonstration. Hard rigour 8 to 12 hours"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 2: Build the landed-cost and margin calculator","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-2","text":"1.2 Build the landed-cost and margin calculator + Calculate per-unit procurement, freight, insurance and other applicable variable costs without double-counting them. Calculate contribution margin and cash required. Document the assumptions behind each calculation, and manually check several examples against the system’s output. Medium rigour 5 to 8 hours"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 3: Build the quotation scenario engine","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-3","text":"1.3 Build the quotation scenario engine + Allow a user to select a product, quantity, destination, delivery basis and target margin. Return a calculated quotation, cost breakdown and expected contribution. Compare supplier pricing tiers and order quantities, flag missing inputs and show when a quotation fails the selected margin requirement. Hard rigour 5 to 8 hours"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 4: Create the distributor and tender-opportunity tracker","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-4","text":"1.4 Create the distributor and tender-opportunity tracker + Track each enquiry through quotation, documentation, purchase order, production and delivery stages. Include requested quantities, next actions, estimated deal value and expected timing. Clearly distinguish confirmed orders from opportunities; label conversion probabilities as assumptions rather than established facts. Medium rigour 5 to 8 hours"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 5: Build the commercial-risk dashboard","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-5","text":"1.5 Build the commercial-risk dashboard + Show supplier, distributor and product concentration, tender dependence, working-capital exposure and outstanding documentation. Define each indicator and explain what it measures. Keep actual sales concentration separate from projected pipeline concentration so the dashboard does not imply revenue that has not occurred. Medium rigour 5 to 8 hours"},{"title":"Phase 1, step 6: Connect everything in an interactive scenario laboratory","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Working commercial system","url":"passion-project.html#phase-1-step-6","text":"1.6 Connect everything in an interactive scenario laboratory + Let users adjust quantities, supplier prices, freight, exchange rates and margins, then see the effects on quotations, contribution and cash requirements. Test the connected workflow with a Solivia stakeholder, check invalid inputs and correct usability problems. Keep confidential information out of the public demonstration. Hard rigour 8 to 12 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 1: Decide whether the data is ready for forecasting","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-1","text":"2.1 Decide whether the data is ready for forecasting + Review completed orders, available history, missing periods, product consistency and lead-time records. Decide which products can support genuine forecast evaluation. Where the evidence is insufficient, defer forecasting or use an explicitly labelled simulation, not a claimed prediction of Solivia’s sales. Medium rigour 4 to 6 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 2: Build and compare demand forecasts","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-2","text":"2.2 Build and compare demand forecasts + Start with simple baselines, such as previous-period demand or a moving average. Add a more complex method only where justified. Evaluate predictions using later observations that were not used to fit the model, examine forecast errors and bias, and explain the model-selection decision. Hard rigour 10 to 16 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 3: Translate uncertainty into inventory rules","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-3","text":"2.3 Translate uncertainty into inventory rules + Estimate plausible demand and lead-time uncertainty. Use this to compare reorder points and safety-stock policies. Explain the trade-off between availability, excess stock and tied-up cash. Distinguish uncertainty estimated from observations from ranges chosen purely for scenario testing. Hard rigour 8 to 12 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 4: Build the constrained procurement optimiser","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-4","text":"2.4 Build the constrained procurement optimiser + Define order quantities as decision variables and choose a clearly stated objective, such as expected contribution after relevant inventory costs. Add supported constraints: budget, minimum order quantities, storage or supplier restrictions. Test whether the recommendations are feasible and whether the model handles cases with no feasible solution. Hard rigour 12 to 20 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 5: Stress-test the procurement policies","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-5","text":"2.5 Stress-test the procurement policies + Compare the optimiser against simpler ordering rules under the same demand and supplier scenarios. Use scenario analysis and, where justified, Monte Carlo simulation to vary demand, delays, freight and tender outcomes. Compare contribution, stockouts, excess inventory and cash exposure, not just forecast accuracy. Hard rigour 10 to 16 hours"},{"title":"Phase 2, step 6: Integrate and validate the recommendations","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Forecasting and procurement optimisation","url":"passion-project.html#phase-2-step-6","text":"2.6 Integrate and validate the recommendations + Add forecasts, inventory rules and procurement comparisons to the application. Show assumptions and uncertainty beside each recommendation. Review results with a Solivia stakeholder, investigate unrealistic outputs and record changes. Distinguish simulated improvements from any benefits observed during actual use. Hard rigour 6 to 10 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 1: Explain the business problem","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Easy roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-1","text":"3.1 Explain the business problem + State which pricing, quotation or procurement decisions the system supports, who will use it and what the project aims to improve. Define the boundaries clearly: this is a commercial decision-support tool, not a clinical recommendation system. Easy rigour 1 to 2 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 2: Describe the existing workflow","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-2","text":"3.2 Describe the existing workflow + Explain how Solivia currently handles supplier information, quotations, enquiries and purchasing decisions. Use stakeholder input and available records to identify genuine difficulties. Do not invent inefficiencies simply to make the new system appear useful. Medium rigour 1 to 2 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 3: Document the data design","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-3","text":"3.3 Document the data design + Explain the database structure, relationships, sources, cleaning decisions and confidentiality measures. Include a compact schema diagram or data dictionary. Identify which fields are observed, estimated or simulated and explain any important missing information. Medium rigour 1 to 2 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 4: Explain the decision methodology","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-4","text":"3.4 Explain the decision methodology + Present the important cost and margin calculations, assumptions and scenario logic. Include forecasting, inventory and optimisation methods only if implemented. Reference methodological sources and justify why the chosen methods fit the available data and business problem. Hard rigour 2 to 3 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 5: Describe the system design","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-5","text":"3.5 Describe the system design + Show how the database, calculation engine and interface connect. Explain the main user workflow, input checks and separation between private company data and the public demonstration. Include a readable architecture diagram and selected screenshots. Medium rigour 1 to 2 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 6: Demonstrate one complete commercial scenario","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-6","text":"3.6 Demonstrate one complete commercial scenario + Walk through an anonymised quotation or procurement decision: inputs → calculations → alternatives → output → interpretation. Explain how changing one important assumption changes the decision. Identify clearly whether the example is real, anonymised or synthetic. Medium rigour 2 to 3 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 7: Evaluate results and business value","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Hard roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-7","text":"3.7 Evaluate results and business value + Report what testing actually established: calculation accuracy, consistent quotations, clearer comparisons, stakeholder feedback or measured task-time changes. Where Phase 2 is completed, report comparisons against baseline policies. Do not claim revenue gains or cost savings without supporting evidence. Hard rigour 2 to 3 hours"},{"title":"Phase 3, step 8: Discuss limitations and finish the report","category":"Passion project","keywords":"Medium roadmap plan Written technical case study","url":"passion-project.html#phase-3-step-8","text":"3.8 Discuss limitations and finish the report + Explain missing data, uncertain assumptions, model weaknesses and remaining development needs. State what is operational versus experimental. Finalise references, captions and reproducibility links; check that every conclusion matches the evidence and that the report contains no confidential information. Medium rigour 2 to 4 hours"},{"title":"Contact Sahana Sharma: GitHub, LinkedIn & email","category":"About","keywords":"contact github sahanasharma email linkedin connect","text":"GitHub: github.com/sahanasharma. LinkedIn: linkedin.com/in/sahana-sharma08. Email: sahana2008lol@gmail.com.","url":"index.html#contact"}];
